Code Quality

Keep any refactor or code additions simple, readable, and uncomplicated.
Keep code inline with the current style of existing code.
Keep things concise.
Combine methods where it makes sense and will not break existing code.
Prefer editing existing functions over creating new ones with similar purpose.
Match existing naming conventions exactly — don't introduce a new casing style or naming pattern.
Don't add comments explaining obvious code; comment only non-obvious logic or decisions.
Avoid premature abstraction — don't generalize code for hypothetical future use cases.

Testing & Validation

When modifying logic, note what should be manually tested afterward.
Don't write tests unless explicitly asked, but flag when a change seems risky enough to warrant one.
